From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 16:40:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] soc/intel/common: Option to set the SMM BIOS write protection
 at finalize

fast_spi_lockdown_cfg() sets EISS, clears WPD and sets the BIOS Lock
Enable at BS_DEV_RESOURCES, with the rest of the chipset lockdown.
With BOOTMEDIA_SMM_BWP on, every flash write ramstage makes after that
fails: the event log starts at BS_POST_DEVICE and the Cannon Lake code
logs the wake and reset events at BS_DEV_INIT, and on a ThinkCentre
M920x each boot with the option on prints "SPI Transaction Error at
Flash Offset c945f5 HSFSTS = 0x0a04f803" and "ELOG: NV Write failed"
for each of them: the boot count, the wake sources and the resets are
never logged, while the entries written from SMM (S3 and S5 entry) are.
The Lynx Point code sets the same bits in the SMI handler at the
finalize, after ramstage is done with the flash.

Add SOC_INTEL_COMMON_PCH_LOCKDOWN_SMM_BWP_LATE: with it, and the SMM
BIOS protection enabled, fast_spi_lockdown_cfg() leaves EISS and the
two lock enables alone and the SMI handler sets them at the finalize,
EISS first, then clears WPD as it already did. Ramstage asks for the
finalize once the payload is loaded, and on S3 resume before the OS
resumes, so the OS never sees the flash writable. The BAR lock, the
VSCC lock and the BIOS interface lock stay where they were. Without
the option nothing changes.

TEST=Not built. On an M920x with the option and BIOS Lock on: no "SPI
Transaction Error" nor "ELOG: NV Write failed" in cbmem -1, the boot
count and the wake source of the boot in the event log, BIOS_SPI_BC
with EISS, LE and WPD 0 read from the OS (tools/update.py protection),
flashrom refusing to write, a capsule update still installing.
---
 src/soc/intel/common/block/smm/smihandler.c  | 10 ++++++++++
 src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/Kconfig    | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/lockdown.c | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 3 files changed, 42 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/soc/intel/common/block/smm/smihandler.c b/src/soc/intel/common/block/smm/smihandler.c
index 5ca77ee9..d282ad82 100644
--- a/src/soc/intel/common/block/smm/smihandler.c
+++ b/src/soc/intel/common/block/smm/smihandler.c
@@ -407,6 +407,16 @@ static void finalize(void)
 	boot_device_init();
 
 	if (enable_smm_bios_protection()) {
+		/*
+		 * With SMM_BWP_LATE the chipset lockdown of ramstage left EISS
+		 * and the BIOS locks to this point, so that ramstage could still
+		 * write the event log: EISS first, then the locks that cover it.
+		 */
+		if (CONFIG(SOC_INTEL_COMMON_PCH_LOCKDOWN_SMM_BWP_LATE)) {
+			fast_spi_set_eiss();
+			fast_spi_set_lock_enable();
+			fast_spi_set_ext_bios_lock_enable();
+		}
 		fast_spi_enable_wp();
 		set_insmm_sts(false);
 	}
diff --git a/src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/Kconfig b/src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/Kconfig
index 38f60d20..309100d6 100644
--- a/src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/Kconfig
+++ b/src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/Kconfig
@@ -7,3 +7,21 @@ config SOC_INTEL_COMMON_PCH_LOCKDOWN
 	  This option allows to have chipset lockdown for DMI, FAST_SPI and
 	  soc_lockdown_config() to implement any additional lockdown as PMC,
 	  LPC for supported PCH.
+
+config SOC_INTEL_COMMON_PCH_LOCKDOWN_SMM_BWP_LATE
+	bool "Apply the SMM BIOS write protection at the SMM finalize"
+	depends on SOC_INTEL_COMMON_PCH_LOCKDOWN && HAVE_SMI_HANDLER
+	depends on BOOTMEDIA_SMM_BWP || BOOTMEDIA_SMM_BWP_RUNTIME_OPTION
+	help
+	  Set EISS, the BIOS Lock Enable and the write protection of the
+	  flash (BIOS_SPI_BC) in the SMI handler at the finalize, which
+	  ramstage asks for after the payload is loaded, instead of at
+	  BS_DEV_RESOURCES with the rest of the chipset lockdown. Between the
+	  two, ramstage still writes the flash: the event log (ELOG), which
+	  starts at BS_POST_DEVICE and logs the wake and reset events at
+	  BS_DEV_INIT, fails every write with the lock already in place
+	  ("SPI Transaction Error", "ELOG: NV Write failed"). The write
+	  protection stays as the OS sees it: set before any boot option or
+	  OS runs, and on S3 resume before the OS resumes. This is the order
+	  of the Lynx Point PCH code. The rest of the SPI lockdown (the
+	  BAR, VSCC, the BIOS interface lock) is unchanged.
diff --git a/src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/lockdown.c b/src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/lockdown.c
index ff7ca959..e898e8cd 100644
--- a/src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/lockdown.c
+++ b/src/soc/intel/common/pch/lockdown/lockdown.c
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 /* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only */
 
 #include <bootstate.h>
+#include <console/console.h>
 #include <intelblocks/cfg.h>
 #include <intelblocks/fast_spi.h>
 #include <intelblocks/lpc_lib.h>
@@ -82,7 +83,19 @@ static void fast_spi_lockdown_cfg(int chipset_lockdown)
 		/* BIOS Interface Lock */
 		fast_spi_set_bios_interface_lock_down();
 
-		/* Only allow writes in SMM */
+		/*
+		 * Only allow writes in SMM. With SMM_BWP_LATE the SMI handler
+		 * sets EISS, the locks and the write protection at the finalize,
+		 * after ramstage has written the event log; until then nothing
+		 * but ramstage runs.
+		 */
+		if (CONFIG(SOC_INTEL_COMMON_PCH_LOCKDOWN_SMM_BWP_LATE) &&
+		    enable_smm_bios_protection()) {
+			printk(BIOS_INFO,
+			       "SPI: BIOS write protection deferred to the SMM finalize\n");
+			return;
+		}
+
 		if (enable_smm_bios_protection()) {
 			fast_spi_set_eiss();
 			fast_spi_enable_wp();
-- 
2.43.0

